# XCU od
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The page fixes neither how many items od writes on a line (a block is a
# multiple of the least common multiple of the type sizes), nor the width
# or zero padding of numbers, nor the letter case of hexadecimal digits,
# nor the byte order of multi-byte items. The tests therefore read the
# output as fields, drop leading zeros, use only hexadecimal values without
# letters (except where both cases are accepted) and use multi-byte items
# whose bytes are equal.

assertion  XCU.od.001
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPTIONS/-A
anchor     utilities/od.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       od -A d, -A o and -A x write at the start of each block the offset
           of its first byte, in decimal, octal or hexadecimal, and after the
           last block a line with only the offset after the last byte and no
           blank after it; -A n writes no offsets.
test       t/001-offsets.sh

assertion  XCU.od.002
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPTIONS/-t
anchor     utilities/od.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       od -t o1, x1, u1 and d1 write each byte as an octal, hexadecimal,
           unsigned decimal or signed decimal number; o2, x2, u2, x4 and x8
           write two, four or eight bytes as one number.
test       t/002-integer-types.sh

assertion  XCU.od.003
anchor     utilities/od.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPTIONS/-t
class      required
since      1992
says       od -t a writes each byte, using only its seven low bits, as the
           ASCII name given for control characters (nul, soh, nl or lf, sp,
           del and so on) or as the printable character itself.
test       t/003-named-characters.sh

assertion  XCU.od.004
anchor     utilities/od.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPTIONS/-t
class      required
since      1992
says       od -t c writes printable bytes as themselves, the C escapes for
           newline, tab, carriage return, backspace, form feed, vertical
           tab and alert, a single backslash for backslash, \0 for NUL and
           three octal digits for other nonprintable bytes.
test       t/004-characters.sh

assertion  XCU.od.005
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPTIONS/-j
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPTIONS/-N
class      required
says       od -j skips that many bytes (decimal, or octal with a leading 0 or
           hexadecimal with 0x) and fails if the input is shorter; od -N
           formats at most that many bytes, a shorter input being no error.
test       t/005-skip-and-count.sh

assertion  XCU.od.006
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPTIONS/-v
class      required
since      1992
says       Without -v, od replaces groups of output lines identical to the
           group before by a line holding only an asterisk (for 1024 equal
           bytes); with -v every group is written.
test       t/006-repeated-lines.sh

assertion  XCU.od.007
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPTIONS/-t
anchor     utilities/od.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       With several types, from several -t options or one type string,
           od writes for each block one line per type, in the order the
           types were given.
test       t/007-several-types.sh

assertion  XCU.od.008
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/od.html#STDIN
anchor     utilities/od.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      1992
says       od treats its file operands as one stream with offsets running on
           across files, reads standard input with no operand, and exits
           nonzero for a file it cannot read.
test       t/008-operands.sh

assertion  XCU.od.009
anchor     utilities/od.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       With no output type od behaves as with -t oS.
test       t/009-default-type.sh

assertion  XCU.od.010
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPTIONS/-b
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPTIONS/-c
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPTIONS/-d
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPTIONS/-o
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPTIONS/-s
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPTIONS/-x
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1992
says       The options -b, -d, -o, -s and -x give the same output as -t o1,
           -t u2, -t o2, -t d2 and -t x2; -c shows the same characters as
           -t c (the page does not state the layouts to be equal).
test       t/010-xsi-letter-options.sh

assertion  XCU.od.011
anchor     utilities/od.html#OPERANDS
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1992
says       On XSI systems, when there are at most two operands, no -A, -j, -N,
           -t or -v is given and the last operand starts with + (or is a
           number after a file name), it is the byte offset at which to
           start, octal unless it ends in a dot.
test       t/011-xsi-offset-operand.sh
